.TH b4-accounts-list 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-accounts\-list \- List accounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 accounts list\fR [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-type\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
List accounts
.PP
List the accounts in the order Banking4 shows them: id, type, name, IBAN (the account number where there is no IBAN), bank (BIC, else bank code), balance with its currency and the day it is from, and the number of bookings.
.PP
Accounts that are hidden in Banking4 are left out unless \-\-all is given.
.PP
The number of bookings counts every booking once: a split booking or a collective booking counts as its parts, not as parts plus the whole.
.PP
The id in the first column names the account wherever a command takes an ACCOUNT.
.PP
With \-\-output json, jsonl or csv every account has these fields: id, kind, name, iban, account_number, bic, bank_code, currency, balance, balance_at, hidden, booking_count, first_booking_date, last_booking_date.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-type\fR \fI<TYPE>\fR
Only accounts of this type; repeat for several types
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bank: Bank account
.IP \(bu 2
cash: Cash
.IP \(bu 2
credit\-card: Credit card
.IP \(bu 2
portfolio: Securities account
.IP \(bu 2
asset: Assets such as real estate
.IP \(bu 2
paypal: PayPal account
.IP \(bu 2
crypto: Crypto currency wallet
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Include accounts that are hidden in Banking4
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 accounts list
    List the visible accounts.
b4 accounts list \-\-all \-\-type bank \-\-type credit\-card
    Bank and credit card accounts, hidden ones included.
b4 accounts list \-\-output csv > accounts.csv
    Write the list to a CSV file.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1),
\fBb4\-accounts\fR(1)
